abstract: "560 tool calls on 7 models from 3 labs (Qwen, DeepSeek, Zhipu) measure whether a reply reports a broken tool or silently passes the corrupted value on. Silent corruption is flagged in 0 replies out of 140, while an announced EACCES failure is disclosed in 87.1%. Declaring the token format in prose inside the tool description lifts catches to 7 out of 70. Asking for a rigid answer format cuts disclosure of loud failures from 91.4% to 37.1%. The judge is deterministic and the call journal is published in full. This is a replication on a minimal task: the niche is covered by the prior work listed in PRIOR-WORK.md."
keywords:
  - "tool use"
  - "agent reliability"
  - "silent failure"
  - "fault injection"
  - "LLM evaluation"
  - "model honesty"
  - "MCP"
